{:edb/db-before-t 2890 :edb/db-after-t 2891 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536203 50 #inst "2026-10-09T20:21:36.775Z" 13194139536203 true] [17592186161645 1100 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n2. $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139536203 false] [17592186161645 1100 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\n- $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139536203 true] [17592186161645 1145 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y$, we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.28$. Likewise,\n$P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.4 \\cdot 0.7 = 0.28. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(0, 1)|= 0.12 = 0.4 \\cdot 0.3 = f_{X}(0) \\cdot f_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1, 0)|= 0.42 = 0.6 \\cdot 0.7 = f_{X}(1) \\cdot f_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1, 1)|= 0.18 = 0.6 \\cdot 0.3 = f_{X}(1) \\cdot f_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536203 false] [17592186161645 1145 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.28.$ Likewise, \n\n$$\nP(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.4 \\cdot 0.7 = 0.28. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =0.12=0.4\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.42=0.6\\cdot 0.7={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.18=0.6\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536203 true] [17592186161646 1100